  "markdown_report": "Choose **exact feasibility recovery for matched NextItNet interventions**. At 20% progress, repairing diagnostic support is better justified than changing temporal weighting from one-user replacement results.\n\nThree starting insights guide this choice. First, displayed replacements retain only one user in Movies and Electronics, and none in Industrial or CDs. These results describe narrow support; failed construction is not a zero effect. Second, terminal-preserving permutations worsen training-target ranks in Industrial and CDs. Earlier ordering influences these examples, but this does not establish that stronger recency weighting would help. Third, strong sampled margins coexist with limited catalog training-prefix hits. These measurements concern different competitors and cannot establish a sampling defect or held-out improvement.\n\nThree related-work insights suggest a practical repair. Bipartite matching distinguishes greedy failure from infeasibility under slotwise constraints [1]. Joint edited-sequence constraints require an additional exact search. Effective-receptive-field research separates possible access from learned influence [2]. Structural reachability should therefore remain a matching control, not a usefulness label. Limited-overlap research restricts interpretation to supported observations [3]. Recovering feasible assignments can expand support without justifying population extrapolation.\n\nThe framework organizes three directions:\n\n| Direction | Existing control | Remaining gap |\n|---|---|---|\n| Construction | Fixed plausibility rules | Greedy versus genuine feasibility failure |\n| Mechanism | Reachability and permutations | Learned pathway contributions |\n| Interpretation | Paired user summaries | Sparse support and selection |\n\nCandidate assessments use originality/potential/difficulty scores, where higher difficulty means harder implementation:\n\n| Idea | Scores | Positive / negative assessment |\n|---|---|---|\n| Exact assignment recovery [1,3] | 4/7/4 | Original integration, reusable support repair, small search; established mathematics, uncertain HR@10 relevance, coupled constraints complicate implementation |\n| Bidirectional residual patching [4] | 5/7/6 | Useful adaptation, mechanism potential, reusable hooks; established method, corruption dependence, architecture-specific bookkeeping |\n| Attribution randomization checks [5] | 2/5/3 | Clear diagnostic addition, detects parameter insensitivity, simple copies; little novelty, no guarantee of faithfulness, added probe cost |\n\nFor the selected idea, freeze the existing candidate pools and constraints before scoring interventions. Construct replacement-slot graphs for each required four-plan block. Compute maximum matching, then search complete assignments subject to every joint constraint; use matching as a pruning bound. Preserve existing reuse rules. Prioritize complete feasible blocks, then minimize a predefined replacement distance with deterministic ties.\n\nRun greedy and exact construction on identical pools. Report rescued blocks/users, graph deficiencies, joint-constraint failures and unresolved timeouts. Compare effects on common support separately from newly recovered support; do not attribute changed averages solely to better estimation. Keep position-preserving edits, fixed/shared-restricted catalogs, exact ranks and user bootstrap. Preserve one NextItNet checkpoint per category and all adapter interfaces; inference remains unchanged. Only pinned four-category validation HR@10 selects model changes. No improvement is claimed.\n\nReferences: [1] SciPy maximum bipartite matching; [2] Luo et al., effective receptive fields; [3] Kallus, generalized optimal matching; [4] activation-patching methodology; [5] Adebayo et al., saliency sanity checks. Links appear in `related_work`.",
  "idea": {
    "description": "Replace greedy construction of position-preserving NextItNet diagnostic replacements with deterministic exact feasibility search on the same frozen admissibility pools. Use bipartite matching to diagnose slotwise feasibility and prune search, then explicitly enforce any joint sequence and cross-plan constraints. Compare greedy and exact construction without training another predictor.",
    "motivation": "The supplied diagnostics show extremely sparse replacement support: displayed dose-one effects in Movies and Electronics each involve only one retained user, while Industrial and CDs expose permutation results but no successful replacement blocks. The existing definitions explicitly acknowledge that greedy failure does not establish infeasibility. Recovering valid assignments under unchanged rules addresses an observed measurement limitation before using replacement sensitivity to motivate training changes. It cannot guarantee additional feasible users or better validation HR@10.",
    "implementation_notes": "Preserve the supplied corrected NextItNet, exactly one independently trained model/checkpoint per category, all four dataset routes, DATA_DIR = os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], trial-local artifacts, preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, maximum 200 epochs and 200 batches per epoch. No ensembles, other-trial checkpoints, extra datasets, pretrained weights, hidden test access or evaluator changes. This proposal changes diagnostic construction only; retain the existing checkpoint and embedded predictor consistently.\n\nRetain DiagnosisProbe(view, device), run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis), and every ModelView interface. The loader remains (None, sequence, positive, negative), with B x L tensors. Preserve existing full-catalog unseen-item recommendations for five real training users per category. Use only available public histories, titles, descriptions and timestamps. Do not infer compatibility, ownership, unmet needs, review evidence or simulator traits.\n\nPreregister the existing users, cuts, doses, lag bins, replacement repetitions, candidate-generation rules, frequency matching, reachability matching, transition constraints and reuse rules. Do not relax constraints or expand pools in response to solver failure or model scores. Hash and serialize each finite pool before either construction method runs. Feasibility conclusions apply to these pools, not to every catalog item. Where temporal availability cannot be established from available timestamps, report that limitation rather than asserting historical availability.\n\nFor each jointly required recent/older and random/constrained four-plan block, represent intervention positions as slots and admissible replacement IDs as edges. Item capacity and uniqueness must follow the existing protocol: allow cross-plan reuse when permitted, and enforce shared capacity only when explicitly required. Ordinary maximum bipartite matching is exact only for the corresponding slotwise graph with those capacities. Use capacity flow or slot-specific item copies when appropriate. Record isolated slots and a deficiency certificate when available; graph feasibility does not establish whole-block feasibility under additional constraints.\n\nUse deterministic depth-first branch-and-bound for the small number of replacement slots. Order slots by remaining admissible count, order candidates by a preregistered training-only distance and item ID, and validate joint constraints as soon as they become decidable. A matching bound on remaining slots can reject branches that cannot complete. Optimize complete-block feasibility first, then total predefined distance. Do not replace failed complete blocks with partial effects. If distance uses embeddings, freeze the trained model and label this model-dependent matching; prefer existing protocol distances to avoid changing the estimand unnecessarily.\n\nPropose a fixed cap of 10,000 search nodes per block and an aggregate synchronized diagnostic cap of 30 seconds per category; these are proposed limits, not measured costs. A timed-out or node-limited block is unresolved unless exhaustive search or a valid infeasibility certificate already establishes its status. A complete feasible incumbent may be scored while its distance optimality remains explicitly unproven. Greedy success with exact-search failure is an implementation warning requiring inspection, not evidence of infeasibility.\n\nRun greedy and exact methods on identical pools and seeds. Separate graph deficiency, exhaustive joint infeasibility, feasible construction, unresolved search and implementation errors. Report eligible users, feasible users, retained blocks, rescued blocks and support by history length and training frequency. Report effects separately on common successful support and on exact-only support; their union changes the supported population. Do not apply unsupported selection weights.\n\nKeep in-place edits, unchanged terminal item, occupied positions and timestamps. Gather the maximum nonpadding index, never count-1; exclude empty histories. Preserve fixed original-prefix eligibility, shared restricted catalogs, inserted-item decomposition, exact chunked ranks with deterministic ties, and existing identity/causality/padding controls. Average repetitions within prefixes and prefixes within users; bootstrap users within categories, with intervals unavailable below two supported users. Compute a four-category macro only when all four categories have defined measurements. Preserve RNG states and individual module modes in finally. Return measured metrics, metric_definitions, raw assignment records, support denominators, unavailable reasons and synchronized cost. All computation counts against the shared 43,200 GPU-second budget. The unchanged host macro validation HR@10 remains authoritative; diagnostic support recovery is not a validation improvement.",
    "pseudocode": "Record protocol, finite-pool hashes, constraints, reuse semantics and solver caps.\nSave RNG states and individual module modes.\ntry:\n    Set the category's single trained NextItNet to evaluation mode.\n    For each existing deterministic training-prefix intervention block:\n        pools = freeze_existing_candidate_pools(block)\n        greedy = run_existing_greedy(pools, unchanged_constraints)\n        graph = build_slot_item_graph(pools, existing_reuse_rules)\n        bound, certificate = capacity_correct_maximum_matching(graph)\n        if bound cannot fill every required slot:\n            exact_status = 'slotwise_infeasible'\n        else:\n            Search assignments deterministically:\n                Select the most constrained remaining slot.\n                Try admissible items in predefined distance/ID order.\n                Reject violated capacity and decidable joint constraints.\n                Prune if remaining matching cannot complete the block.\n                Retain complete valid assignments; minimize fixed distance.\n                Stop at preregistered node/time caps.\n            Distinguish exhaustive infeasibility, feasible incumbent,\n            proven optimum and unresolved search.\n        Log assignments, certificates, failures, node counts and cost.\n        Score complete valid greedy/exact plans only:\n            Preserve positions, padding, terminal item and candidate eligibility.\n            Encode each unique history once; gather maximum nonpadding index.\n            Stream exact ranks on fixed and shared restricted catalogs.\n    Report construction outcomes and rescued users/blocks.\n    Summarize paired effects on common support and exact-only support separately.\n    Average within prefix, then user; bootstrap users within category.\nfinally:\n    Restore RNG states and every saved module mode.\nReturn metrics, metric_definitions, raw records and measured cost.\nAggregate equal-category summaries only when all four results are defined.",
    "originality": {
      "score": 4,
      "positive": "Combines exact assignment recovery, joint-block feasibility and support-specific effect reporting for the existing NextItNet intervention adapter.",
      "negative": "Matching, constraint search and complete-case reporting are established methods; the contribution is a targeted diagnostic integration."
    },
    "future_potential": {
      "score": 7,
      "positive": "Can determine whether sparse intervention support reflects construction failure or restrictive candidate pools, supporting more defensible later single-model experiments.",
      "negative": "Exact search may recover little support, and recovered training-prefix sensitivity has no established relationship with benchmark validation gains."
    },
    "code_difficulty": {
      "score": 4,
      "positive": "Doses one and two create small assignment problems, existing candidate pools are reusable, and inference needs no modification.",
      "negative": "Correct capacity semantics, coupled constraints, timeout classification and support-aware aggregation require careful implementation."
    }
  },
  "related_work": [
